  [1mMR101 · create-index-non-concurrent[m

  [1mWhat it detects[m

  CREATE INDEX or CREATE UNIQUE INDEX without CONCURRENTLY on a table that this change set didn't
  create.

  [1mWhy it's dangerous[m

  Building an index reads the whole table. Without CONCURRENTLY, Postgres holds a SHARE lock on the
  table for the entire build. Reads still work, but every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE waits. On a
  table with millions of rows the build can take minutes, and the application's write requests queue
  up behind it.

  [1mLock & impact[m

  Statement: CREATE INDEX
  Lock: SHARE
  Blocks: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
  Work: full table scan and index build

  Statement: CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY
  Lock: SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE
  Blocks: other schema changes and VACUUM
  Work: two table scans, waits for older transactions

  These locks apply to PostgreSQL 12 through 18.

  [1mHow to fix[m

  Add CONCURRENTLY and run the statement outside a transaction. Postgres rejects CREATE INDEX
  CONCURRENTLY inside a transaction block, so turn off the migration tool's transaction for this
  migration.

    CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_status ON orders (status);

  If a concurrent build fails, it leaves an INVALID index behind. Drop it with DROP INDEX
  CONCURRENTLY and run the migration again.

  [1mWhen it's safe to ignore[m

  •  The table is empty or very small in every environment.
  •  The migration runs during a maintenance window with writes stopped.
  •  The table is partitioned. Postgres can't build an index on a partitioned table concurrently.
     Create the index concurrently on each partition, then create it on the parent with ON ONLY and
     attach the partition indexes.

  [1mExamples[m

  Bad:

    CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status ON orders (status);

  Good:

    CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_status ON orders (status);

  [1mReferences[m

  •  PostgreSQL: Building indexes concurrently
     (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html#SQL-CREATEINDEX-CONCURRENTLY)
  •  PostgreSQL: Table-level lock modes
     (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html#LOCKING-TABLES)
